img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;An old tile stove&quot; by freestocks.org, CC0, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Three strategies for shaded homes<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-feature-row is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png\" alt=\"technician - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1756\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-technician-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Load-aware sizing<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Precise Manual J calculations that account for actual tree cover and sun exposure on your specific lot.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1.png\" alt=\"minisplit - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1765\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Multi-zone comfort<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Mini-split systems with separate controls for rooms in full sun and shaded spaces.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-feature-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-medium s-feature-card__img\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-2.png\" alt=\"minisplit - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1766\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-2.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-2-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-2-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-minisplit-2-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Humidity first<\/h3>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Whole-house dehumidification so shaded homes stay dry, not just cool.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png\" alt=\"condenser - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1767\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-condenser-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gemini-2.5-flash-image)\">AI<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How tree shade changes your cooling load<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">When an HVAC contractor performs a Manual J load calculation, the software asks for square footage, insulation, windows, and orientation. One of the optional inputs is shading\u2014light, medium, or heavy\u2014but most contractors just leave it at the default without walking the site. In Media, where tree cover varies block by block, that shortcut leads to wildly inaccurate results.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A home on the north side of West Street with mature oaks overhead might have heavy shading on the roof and all south-facing windows, reducing solar heat gain by 60 to 80 percent. A home on the south side of the same street with no trees will have peak cooling loads 40 to 50 percent higher, even if the homes are identical in size and construction.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The problem is compounded because tree shade isn&#039;t constant. The canopy density changes as trees grow, as limbs are trimmed, and as trees die or are removed. A home that had heavy shade ten years ago might have moderate shade now because the sycamore in the front yard came down in a storm.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you&#039;re installing a new system, you need to base sizing on current conditions, but you also need to think about what might change in the next ten to fifteen years. If your neighbor&#039;s aging maple is likely to come down soon, you could be in full sun within years, and your undersized AC won&#039;t keep up.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zoning for variable sun exposure<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">One of the best ways to handle cooling load variation across a Media home is to use a multi-zone mini-split system. Instead of a single central AC unit treating the whole house as one zone, you install separate indoor heads in the rooms with the highest loads\u2014typically west-facing bedrooms, sunrooms, and spaces without tree shade.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Each head has its own thermostat, so the room in full sun can run at full capacity while the shaded living room stays comfortable with minimal cooling. This approach is more efficient than oversizing a single unit to handle the worst-case room, and it gives occupants better control over comfort. Multi-zone systems work well for homes where tree canopy creates dramatic differences between floors.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In a two-story home with a shaded first floor and an exposed second floor\u2014common in Media&#039;s older neighborhoods\u2014the upstairs bedrooms might need twice the cooling capacity of the downstairs living spaces. A single-zone system will either overcool the first floor or undercool the second floor. A multi-zone system lets you put more capacity upstairs and less downstairs, matching equipment to the actual load in each zone.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some systems let you shut off unused zones, which saves energy and reduces compressor wear.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg\" alt=\"hvac technician repair\" class=\"wp-image-188\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;AC Service&quot; by SuperTech HVAC Services, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The humidity paradox<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Tree shade reduces cooling loads, but it doesn&#039;t reduce humidity. In fact, shaded homes in Media can be more humid than their sunny neighbors because cooler surfaces don&#039;t evaporate moisture as quickly and the HVAC system doesn&#039;t run as often. If your AC is sized for a shaded house, it might only run a few hours a day during mild summer weather\u2014not enough runtime to dehumidify indoor air.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The result is a home that feels cool but damp, with that sticky sensation that makes you want to lower the thermostat even though the temperature is already comfortable.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Solutions for persistent dampness<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Shaded Media homes need active humidity management, not just cooling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-icon-list\">\n<li>Whole-house dehumidifier ties into your ductwork and removes moisture independently of cooling, maintaining 45\u201350% relative humidity even when AC isn&#039;t running.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Mini-split dry mode runs the indoor fan at low speed to maximize moisture removal, though it&#039;s not a substitute for dedicated dehumidification in persistently damp homes.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Air-sealing your home through weatherstripping and caulking limits moist outdoor air infiltration, especially important in older homes with leaky windows and doors.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Bathroom and kitchen exhaust fans vent moisture at the source before it spreads through the house, reducing the burden on your dehumidification system.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Standalone dehumidifiers in basements or main living areas help when ductwork isn&#039;t available, though they require regular reservoir emptying or drain line installation.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-overlap has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-10371125 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-overlap__img has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large alignwide\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-furnace-1-1024x683.png\" alt=\"furnace - professional HVAC service photo\" class=\"wp-image-1477\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-furnace-1-1024x683.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-furnace-1-300x200.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-furnace-1-768x512.png 768w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-furnace-1.png 1536w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--ai\" title=\"AI-generated (gpt-image-1)\">AI<\/span><\/figure>\n<\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-overlap__card has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-bfed0f9d wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:2.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:2.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Future-proof your sizing<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">HVAC equipment is typically sized to match current load as closely as possible\u2014a right-sized system runs more efficiently and provides better humidity control than an oversized one. But in Media, where tree cover is subject to change, there&#039;s an argument for sizing slightly larger than the Manual J calculation suggests.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If your home is heavily shaded now but the trees are mature and approaching the end of their lifespan, you could be in full sun within five years, and your perfectly sized AC will be undersized for new conditions. Adding 10 to 15 percent to equipment capacity now gives you a buffer that won&#039;t hurt efficiency much but will save you from premature replacement if the tree situation changes.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Variable-capacity heat pumps and AC systems can modulate output based on demand, running at lower speeds when the load is light\u2014like when trees provide shade\u2014and ramping up to full capacity when needed. This gives you efficient operation under normal conditions and the ability to handle higher loads without being locked into a fixed-capacity unit that&#039;s either too big or too small.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Variable-capacity systems cost more up front, but in a situation where future conditions are uncertain, they offer adaptability that traditional equipment can&#039;t match.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Media&#039;s neighborhood variations<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-3 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Downtown State Street<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Dense, mature canopy on narrow lots. Homes shaded by their own trees and neighbors&#039; trees. Lower cooling loads, limited winter solar gain.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rose Tree\/Middletown areas<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Larger lots with more open space and variable tree cover. Some blocks heavily wooded, others more suburban. Mixed cooling and heating loads.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Bortondale near trolley<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Older rowhouses and bungalows with limited yard space and less tree cover overall. Higher exposure means higher and more consistent cooling demands.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--warm-gradient has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills\">\n<li>State Street rowhouses: 16\u201318ft wide, single-zone ductless covers entire floor<\/li>\n\n\n<li>PECO summer peak rates hit $0.18\/kWh \u2014 right-sized equipment avoids penalty cycling<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Pre-war plaster walls: fish-tape routing for mini-split wiring avoids surface conduit<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Shared-wall homes: 30\u201340% less heating than detached homes of equal square footage<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Our sizing process for Media homes<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Walk the lot \u2014 We observe tree canopy, sun exposure, and lot orientation at different times of day.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Precise Manual J calculation \u2014 Window-by-window shading inputs, accurate duct routing, and realistic ventilation loads for your specific home.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Zone and dehumidify \u2014 We recommend mini-split zoning for variable loads and match dehumidification to your microclimate.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Account for change \u2014 We size equipment to handle current conditions while building in buffer for tree loss or growth.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Verify performance \u2014 After installation, we test actual comfort and efficiency to confirm the system matches the design.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Rowhouse efficiency self-check<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Shared-wall side: warm and comfortable \u2014 party wall insulating naturally<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Exposed-wall side: cold in winter, hot in summer \u2014 all heat loss is directional<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Front-to-back temperature difference exceeds 5\u00b0F \u2014 duct distribution favoring one end<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Basement stays damp May through October \u2014 foundation moisture affecting equipment<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Electric bill jumps $80+ in summer vs. spring \u2014 possible AC sizing or duct leakage<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Questions about Media&#039;s shaded microclimates<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>My house is heavily shaded\u2014can I install a smaller AC than the calculator suggests?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Possibly, but only if the load calculation was done correctly with accurate shading inputs for your specific lot. Most online calculators don&#039;t account for heavy shade, so they overestimate cooling load. A properly performed Manual J calculation with your actual tree cover will give you a more accurate size. Be cautious about undersizing too aggressively\u2014if tree cover changes or you have west-facing windows in full sun, a too-small unit won&#039;t keep up during heat waves.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my shaded house feel humid even when the AC is running?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Because the cooling load is low, your AC doesn&#039;t run long enough to remove moisture from the air. Air conditioners dehumidify as a byproduct of cooling, but they need runtime to do it effectively. In a shaded home, the temperature might be comfortable with only an hour or two of AC per day, but that&#039;s not enough to control humidity.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A whole-house dehumidifier or a mini-split with dedicated dehumidification will solve the problem by removing moisture independently of the cooling cycle.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Should I size my system for current tree cover or assume the trees might come down?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">It depends on the age and condition of the trees. If they&#039;re mature and healthy, size for current conditions. If they&#039;re aging or in decline, consider adding 10 to 15 percent to your equipment capacity to account for the possibility that you&#039;ll lose shade in the next few years. A modest oversize won&#039;t hurt efficiency much, and it gives you a buffer if conditions change.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Variable-capacity systems are another option\u2014they modulate output to match current loads while still having reserve capacity if sun exposure increases.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Is a mini-split system right for my Media rowhouse?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Very often, yes. State Street rowhouses and similar properties 16\u201318 feet wide are ideal for single-zone ductless systems. One outdoor compressor, one or two indoor heads, and individual room control without the complexity of duct routing through shallow basements. They&#039;re especially effective when the cooling load is variable because of tree shade on one side and exposure on the other. The only caveat is dehumidification\u2014make sure your mini-split has a robust dry mode or pair it with a dedicated dehumidifier.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-1 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" data-id=\"1475\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez-technician-1-1024x